      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So if I'm understanding correctly, the drug basically acts like a fake substrate that sits in the enzyme's seat so the real one can't get in. Could you explain a bit more about the physiological consequences of that \"blockade\" on the rest of the RAAS cascade? Specifically, what happens to the levels of bradykinin in this process, and why does that affect things like cough as a side effect?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You’re on the right track; the body perceives Lisinopril as its substrate imitator, allowing us to continue with your analogy. As you astutely pointed out, one of the by-products of ACE inhibition is an increase in bradykinin, a peptide that causes blood vessels to dilate (vasodilation). ACE also breaks down bradykinin normally, so when you inhibit ACE, bradykinin levels accumulate. While bradykinin is beneficial for blood vessel dilation and lowering blood pressure, high levels can also stimulate pain receptors, leading to angioedema, an unpleasant swelling. Notably, bradykinin is also responsible for stimulating cough receptors, which can lead to a dry, persistent cough that is a common side effect of ACE inhibitors. This cough side effect might not be as common with Angiotensin II Receptor Blockers (ARBs), another class of blood pressure-lowering drugs that your board might also cover. In summary, while ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril are effective at lowering blood pressure, they also engage in a series of off-target effects due to their interaction with other systems influenced by the RAAS, like the central you now understand the notion of ACE functioning as a",
